WEBVTT


1
00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:15.420
So the first and older and actually very successful and impactful way of doing AI-assisted theorem proving is Sledgehammer, so-called Sledgehammer.

2
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:28.970
So Sledgehammer was developed in 2010 by Larry Paulson and Jasmin Blanchette, both are two of the core Isabelle developers in 2010.

3
00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:34.970
And it basically bridges interactive theorem provers like Isabelle with external automated theorem provers.

4
00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:38.970
So these are called SMT solvers for instance, so first order logic solvers.

5
00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:46.510
I mean, we don't even need to go into what type of external theorem provers are used.

6
00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:51.150
It's all what you need to know is really the general architecture.

7
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:55.250
So what happens here is that we have a goal that we want to prove in Isabelle.

8
00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:09.730
And the system, the Sledgehammer, the way it works is that it selects some existing facts using a basic machine learning filter.

9
00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:16.730
So that, for example, if you want to prove something about lists, if you want to prove something about filters on lists,

10
00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:23.730
then it chooses all the lemmas that are relevant that seem related to filters on lists.

11
00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:32.860
So lemmas that take filter and lemmas that take maybe concatenation and filter and mapping and filter and so forth.

12
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.860
So that's the first step.

13
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:42.050
And then after it gets all those theorems, all those facts about lists and filters or the relevant facts,

14
00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:50.460
it moves, it passes them along with the goal that you want to prove to an external automated theorem prover, ATP.

15
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:59.460
And that external theorem prover will try to prove your goal using the facts that were selected by this machine learning based filter.

16
00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:07.460
And then afterwards, after the fact, after the external theorem prover finish, if it is successful,

17
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.040
it gives you, okay, this is my proof. And these are actually the lemmas that I needed.

18
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.040
I didn't need all the facts that you filtered. I only needed some minimal lemmas.

19
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:25.040
And then the proof from that external theorem prover is again reconstructed inside Isabelle,

20
00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.040
because Isabelle actually does not trust external theorem provers because they might have bugs in them.

21
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:36.040
So what we do, so basically the process is you want to prove an Isabelle sub-goal, use Sledgehammer.

22
00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:43.040
How does Sledgehammer work? Use machine learning to set up some facts that seem relevant to proving this goal,

23
00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.040
then sends those facts along with the goal to an external theorem prover.

24
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:53.040
And then if the external theorem prover succeeds, we take its proof and try to redo it again inside Isabelle

25
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.040
so that it becomes an Isabelle proof rather than external theorem prover's proof.

26
00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:13.140
And this approach really has a great advantage, which is that it blends the best of both worlds.

27
00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:18.140
So you have the efficiency of all these external theorem provers, which could solve goals

28
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:24.140
that cannot be solved by O2 or fast-force or same for any of the internal methods.

29
00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.140
But at the same time, you still have the trustworthiness of Isabelle's kernel.

30
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:38.580
And I will just do a very short demo for you as to how to use Sledgehammer to prove a goal.
